   "answer": "All four plan steps are now done. The final answer is below.\n\n1. Financial aid is linked to a lower re-arrest rate after adjustment: hazard ratio 0.68 (95% CI 0.47 to 0.996; p = 0.047, Cox model, two-sided, alpha 0.05). The result is borderline, and the model does not show a causal effect.\n\n| Measure | Value | 95% CI | n (one n = one released man) | Test | Step |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hazard ratio, fin (aid vs no aid) | 0.684 | 0.47 to 0.996 | 432 men, 114 re-arrested | Cox model, Efron ties, Wald p | fit_cox |\n| p value, fin | 0.047 | — | 432 men | Cox model Wald test, two-sided | fit_cox |\n| Concordance (separation of early and late arrests) | 0.640 | — | 432 men, 114 events | Harrell concordance, in sample | fit_cox |\n| Proportional hazards test, fin | p = 0.90 | — | 432 men | Proportional hazards test | fit_cox |\n| Proportional hazards test, age | p = 0.0007 (Holm rejects) | — | 432 men | Proportional hazards test, Holm over 7 tests | fit_cox, adjust_pvalues |\n| Proportional hazards test, wexp | p = 0.007 (Holm rejects) | — | 432 men | Proportional hazards test, Holm over 7 tests | fit_cox, adjust_pvalues |\n\nUncertain:\n- The proportional hazards assumption fails for age and wexp. Their hazard ratios are not constant over time.\n- The concordance of 0.640 is in sample only. It shows modest separation of early and late arrests.\n- The file does not say how aid was assigned. The adjusted estimate is an association, not proof of a causal effect.\n\nWaits for you:\nNothing.\n\nSettings used, from the decision record: Significance level (alpha): 0.05 · Correction for a list of p-values: holm · Covariates of the Cox model: fin,age,race,wexp,mar,paro,prio · Ties method of the Cox model: efron · Reference level of each categorical covariate: first.",
